    "['l2 binding vsi [vsi-name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching interface-mode trunk vlan members 2', 'parent_command': ['interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1'], 'root': 'interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching interface-mode trunk vlan members 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching vlan members ldp1\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for Juniper's L2VPN VPLS context:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 encapsulation vlan-bridge\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 vlan-id 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family bridge interface-mode trunk\\nset routing-instances ldp1 interface ge-1/0/1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent depends on Juniper's VPLS configuration style. The first approach shows Ethernet switching binding, while the second shows a more direct VPLS instance attachment. Juniper typically uses routing-instances for VPLS rather than direct interface binding to VSIs.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching interface-mode trunk vlan members 2'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching vlan members ldp1', 'parent_command': ['interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1'], 'root': 'interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching interface-mode trunk vlan members 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching vlan members ldp1\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for Juniper's L2VPN VPLS context:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 encapsulation vlan-bridge\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 vlan-id 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family bridge interface-mode trunk\\nset routing-instances ldp1 interface ge-1/0/1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent depends on Juniper's VPLS configuration style. The first approach shows Ethernet switching binding, while the second shows a more direct VPLS instance attachment. Juniper typically uses routing-instances for VPLS rather than direct interface binding to VSIs.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching vlan members ldp1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 encapsulation vlan-bridge', 'parent_command': ['interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1'], 'root': 'interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching interface-mode trunk vlan members 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching vlan members ldp1\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for Juniper's L2VPN VPLS context:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 encapsulation vlan-bridge\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 vlan-id 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family bridge interface-mode trunk\\nset routing-instances ldp1 interface ge-1/0/1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent depends on Juniper's VPLS configuration style. The first approach shows Ethernet switching binding, while the second shows a more direct VPLS instance attachment. Juniper typically uses routing-instances for VPLS rather than direct interface binding to VSIs.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 encapsulation vlan-bridge'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 vlan-id 2', 'parent_command': ['interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1'], 'root': 'interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching interface-mode trunk vlan members 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching vlan members ldp1\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for Juniper's L2VPN VPLS context:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 encapsulation vlan-bridge\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 vlan-id 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family bridge interface-mode trunk\\nset routing-instances ldp1 interface ge-1/0/1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent depends on Juniper's VPLS configuration style. The first approach shows Ethernet switching binding, while the second shows a more direct VPLS instance attachment. Juniper typically uses routing-instances for VPLS rather than direct interface binding to VSIs.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 vlan-id 2'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family bridge interface-mode trunk', 'parent_command': ['interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1'], 'root': 'interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching interface-mode trunk vlan members 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching vlan members ldp1\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for Juniper's L2VPN VPLS context:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 encapsulation vlan-bridge\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 vlan-id 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family bridge interface-mode trunk\\nset routing-instances ldp1 interface ge-1/0/1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent depends on Juniper's VPLS configuration style. The first approach shows Ethernet switching binding, while the second shows a more direct VPLS instance attachment. Juniper typically uses routing-instances for VPLS rather than direct interface binding to VSIs.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family bridge interface-mode trunk'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances ldp1 interface ge-1/0/1.1', 'parent_command': ['interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1'], 'root': 'interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching interface-mode trunk vlan members 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family ethernet-switching vlan members ldp1\\n##\\n\\nOr more precisely for Juniper's L2VPN VPLS context:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 encapsulation vlan-bridge\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 vlan-id 2\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 family bridge interface-mode trunk\\nset routing-instances ldp1 interface ge-1/0/1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The exact equivalent depends on Juniper's VPLS configuration style. The first approach shows Ethernet switching binding, while the second shows a more direct VPLS instance attachment. Juniper typically uses routing-instances for VPLS rather than direct interface binding to VSIs.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances ldp1 interface ge-1/0/1.1'}]]": 1,
    "['interface [interface-name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet address 10.1.1.2/24', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet address 10.1.1.2/24', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `interface GigabitEthernet1/0/2`:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet address 10.1.1.2/24\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family mpls\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent maintains the same interface addressing and MPLS configuration, though Juniper uses a different syntax structure with `unit 0` and separates the address family configurations. The `undo shutdown` is implicit in Juniper (interfaces are enabled by default). The OSPF and MPLS LDP configurations would need to be configured separately in the protocols hierarchy in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet address 10.1.1.2/24'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family mpls', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family mpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `interface GigabitEthernet1/0/2`:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet address 10.1.1.2/24\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family mpls\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent maintains the same interface addressing and MPLS configuration, though Juniper uses a different syntax structure with `unit 0` and separates the address family configurations. The `undo shutdown` is implicit in Juniper (interfaces are enabled by default). The OSPF and MPLS LDP configurations would need to be configured separately in the protocols hierarchy in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family mpls'}]]": 1,
